1. Executive Conclusion

TENTORA is a viable and differentiated creative-technology concept, but its strength depends on disciplined execution rather than feature quantity.

The platform addresses a recognizable problem: project owners may know what creative outcome they need, yet struggle to translate their taste into a useful brief, identify the right creative professional, maintain consistency across multiple services, and manage the work through delivery. Existing alternatives often solve only one part of that journey.

TENTORA proposes one connected system with two paths:

Design Now: a fast self-service path using curated, creator-authored designs that customers personalize only through approved properties.

Work With a Creative: a guided path that converts visual choices into a reusable Main Taste Profile and recommends no more than six relevant creatives, studios, or multidisciplinary teams.

The differentiating idea is not “AI creates art.” It is that AI helps structure taste, control safe customization, improve matching, preserve consistency, and organize the transition from a ready design to professional creative work—while human creators retain authorship and control.

The recommended decision is to proceed with a controlled MVP, subject to four conditions:

Validate the most important user assumptions with real project owners and creatives.

Deliver one complete vertical slice for each product path before expanding catalogue breadth.

Secure creator rights, licences, and usable curated content before publishing Ready Designs.

Treat all performance, relevance, adoption, and impact targets as proposed until measured.

2. The Problem

2.1 Client problem

Project owners commonly face five connected difficulties:

They understand their business need but may not know the correct creative service.

They browse many portfolios without knowing which style fits their project.

They struggle to express visual taste in a conventional written brief.

Multi-service projects can become fragmented across unrelated creatives and tools.

Messages, files, feedback, approvals, and deliveries become difficult to track.

2.2 Creative problem

Creatives also face structural problems:

Opportunities may be poorly matched to their genuine style or experience.

Portfolio discovery often rewards volume and popularity rather than relevance.

Repetitive enquiries consume time before project fit is known.

Creator-authored reusable designs lack a controlled commercialization and licensing layer.

Attribution, modification limits, AI permissions, and compensation can become unclear.

2.3 Market gap to validate

The project hypothesis is that clients do not need another unlimited freelance marketplace or generic template library. They need a curated route from sector and need to a suitable result, with visual guidance and reduced choice overload.

This hypothesis is plausible but must be validated through interviews, usability tests, and pilot transactions. The current documents establish the concept; they do not yet prove demand, willingness to pay, acquisition cost, or retention.

