6. End-to-End Product Experience

6.1 Shared entry

Sector → Service → Visual Examples → Choose Product Path

Starting from the sector reduces the need for clients to understand creative terminology before exploring solutions.

6.2 Path A — Design Now

Sector → Service → Ready Design → Controlled Customization → Preview → Save or Export

The user selects a curated design made by a verified creator or studio. The creator defines which properties may change, such as:

Text and content.

Approved color values or palettes.

Font choices.

Images and image treatments.

Layout variants.

Dimensions and export formats.

Controlled repeaters, such as menu items.

AI may assist with approved actions such as rewriting, translation, image cleanup, background removal, color suggestions, content arrangement, or resizing. Every action must remain within the Ready Design schema, be previewed, and preserve the creator’s artistic boundaries.

6.3 Path B — Work With a Creative

Sector → One or More Services → My Project Needs → Taste Discovery → Main Taste Profile → Optional Service Direction → Up to Six Recommendations → Selection → Workspace → Delivery

The client completes one visual test for the project rather than repeating a separate test for every service. The Main Taste Profile represents weighted preferences such as style, atmosphere, color, composition, material, form, and visual direction.

A service-specific direction is stored as a limited adjustment to the project’s main taste. It must not replace or fragment the shared project direction.

6.4 Connected handoff

Saved Design Session → Continue With a Creative → Immutable Handoff Snapshot → Match or Select → Professional Development

The handoff preserves the selected Ready Design version, property values, text, uploaded assets, previews, AI actions, creator attribution, licence reference, and a structured summary of the customer’s choices.

The client therefore continues the same journey instead of restarting the brief.

7. Taste Discovery and Creative DNA

7.1 Main Taste Profile

The MVP should derive the profile from curated visual selections with reviewed style tags. It should be understandable to the user and reusable across the project.

The profile is a decision aid, not an objective psychological diagnosis. Results must avoid claims of perfect or scientifically guaranteed taste detection.

7.2 Creative DNA

Creative DNA represents a creative’s actual working identity using evidence such as:

Approved portfolio work.

Services offered.

Style and medium tags.

Relevant sector experience.

Capacity to cover one or several services.

Curator review and publication status.

Creative DNA must be versioned and based on real approved evidence. Creatives should be able to review how their work is represented.

7.3 Explainable matching

The initial matching system should combine transparent factors:

Required-service coverage.

Taste similarity.

Sector relevance.

Portfolio evidence.

Availability or capacity, when verified.

Multi-service coverage.

Team compatibility when no single creative covers the project.

The system returns no more than six options. Each option must explain the main reasons it was recommended. Matching weights remain configurable and require evaluation before they become an approved formula.

8. Artificial Intelligence Strategy

8.1 Appropriate AI responsibilities

AI can add value in six bounded areas:

Structuring visual selections into a Taste Profile.

Assisting with permitted Ready Design properties.

Supporting portfolio tagging and Creative DNA review.

Ranking compatible creatives or teams.

Maintaining consistency across project services.

Summarizing project information and preserving handoff context.

8.2 Guardrails

AI cannot modify a property outside the published Ready Design schema.

Users must preview and confirm material changes.

Portfolio and Ready Design assets cannot be used for model training without separate explicit consent.

Provider, model version, action type, result, and user acceptance should be logged.

Failed AI actions require a safe deterministic fallback.

Internal scores must not be presented as guarantees.

Human creators retain authorship of original creative work.

8.3 Technical innovation claim

TENTORA’s innovation is the orchestration of visual taste, creator evidence, controlled design adaptation, explainable matching, and project continuity. The credible claim is not that any individual algorithm is unprecedented; it is that these components are combined to solve a cultural and creative-production workflow coherently.

9. Human Creativity, Rights, and Trust

Creator rights are a product requirement, not an administrative afterthought.

Every published Ready Design should include:

Verified creator identity.

Ownership and source-asset records.

Editable-property boundaries.

Permitted and prohibited AI actions.

Licence type and commercial-use terms.

Attribution requirements.

Compensation model.

Design and licence version history.

Separate model-training consent, where applicable.

Publication must be blocked when mandatory rights fields are incomplete. Historical sessions, exports, and licences must remain traceable even if a design is later unpublished.

10. Brand and Experience Direction

The name TENTORA combines Tentacle and Aurora:

Tentacle represents several creative capabilities connected to one coordinated vision.

Aurora represents color, artistic diversity, movement, and visual wonder.

The concept should be expressed abstractly through flexible connecting lines, controlled motion, selective color fields, modular clusters, and relationships between project images. Literal octopus illustrations and full-interface rainbow gradients should be avoided.

Experience principles

Artistic, expressive, contemporary, and memorable.

Simple enough for non-designers to understand quickly.

Work-led, with large authentic project imagery.

Neutral interface so creative projects provide most of the color.

Editorial hierarchy and an asymmetric but controlled grid.

Clear calls to action and restrained micro-interactions.

Arabic-first RTL architecture with an English option when localized.

Mobile-first, keyboard-accessible, and compatible with reduced motion.

No generic SaaS-dashboard appearance, excessive glassmorphism, or empty decorative effects.

